{
  "dom_id": "step-q120109",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/3d24906da8cd181d40dbaec0095b8d51481babc152609fc378b03c61187220c3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7f8a042b9944b2cdf0e864b7dca760f094f4413f197befa05d6e5cbefb3841ba.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
